[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [[:db/retract 17592186058650 :answer-field/choices 17592186058653] {:db/id 17592186059840 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-29efc143-8ef6-514b-b08b-b09cf3c6dccf"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059515 :question/problem "Find the value of $\\sin{270^\\circ}.$" :question/worked-solution "The angle $270^\\circ$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $270^\\circ$ corresponds to the point $(0,-1).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\sin{270^\\circ} = -1.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059516 :question/problem "Find the value of $\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "The angle $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,-1).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) = 0.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) = -1.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\tan{\\theta} = \\dfrac{\\sin \\theta}{\\cos\\theta},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right)}{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right)} = \\dfrac{-1}{0}.\n$$\n\nHowever, division by zero is undefined. Therefore, $\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right)$ is undefined." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059842 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-621a7d94-aa15-5da2-a86e-6e37bead83f1" "question-5dc0c374-75b0-5038-a236-6349d3712634"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059517 :question/problem "Find the value of $\\csc \\left(0\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "The angle $0$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/ed/edad8c833f65c54453fe40a326cbf9394c263213934447872b530a8e4fb86de1.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $0$ corresponds to the point $(1,0).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\sin{\\left(0\\right)} = 0.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\csc{\\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\csc{\\left(0\\right)} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{\\left(0\\right)}} = \\dfrac{1}{0}.\n$$\n\nHowever, division by zero is undefined. Therefore, $\\csc\\left(0\\right)$ is undefined." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186060255 :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡({90}^{∘})=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The angle ${90}^{∘}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/ed/edad8c833f65c54453fe40a326cbf9394c263213934447872b530a8e4fb86de1.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${90}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({90}^{∘})=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186058167 :answer-field/correct "answer-8f074172-16a6-5de2-9d7b-00f0f7fba825" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8f074172-16a6-5de2-9d7b-00f0f7fba825"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f074172-16a6-5de2-9d7b-00f0f7fba825" :answer/id #uuid "8f074172-16a6-5de2-9d7b-00f0f7fba825" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-29efc143-8ef6-514b-b08b-b09cf3c6dccf" :question/id #uuid "29efc143-8ef6-514b-b08b-b09cf3c6dccf" :question/math-academy-id "q-64861" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "The angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d78da839-c447-5f6a-b9f4-52fee3e7c783"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d78da839-c447-5f6a-b9f4-52fee3e7c783" :answer-field/id #uuid "d78da839-c447-5f6a-b9f4-52fee3e7c783" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6238dcb6-e730-5761-a8fc-314e6a8b5732" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bc748a1d-aa55-53b1-8727-38e8aeaf186e" "answer-5e88955e-5bc1-5000-8512-3eeafc61d0f6" "answer-6238dcb6-e730-5761-a8fc-314e6a8b5732" "answer-751d8865-5818-58ec-a5f2-b7595be506a7" "answer-a54b68c4-6f83-5b6d-a8d5-c61dfd48387e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc748a1d-aa55-53b1-8727-38e8aeaf186e" :answer/id #uuid "bc748a1d-aa55-53b1-8727-38e8aeaf186e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e88955e-5bc1-5000-8512-3eeafc61d0f6" :answer/id #uuid "5e88955e-5bc1-5000-8512-3eeafc61d0f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6238dcb6-e730-5761-a8fc-314e6a8b5732" :answer/id #uuid "6238dcb6-e730-5761-a8fc-314e6a8b5732" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-751d8865-5818-58ec-a5f2-b7595be506a7" :answer/id #uuid "751d8865-5818-58ec-a5f2-b7595be506a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a54b68c4-6f83-5b6d-a8d5-c61dfd48387e" :answer/id #uuid "a54b68c4-6f83-5b6d-a8d5-c61dfd48387e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Undefined" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186060256 :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡({180}^{∘}).$\n\n$\\operatorname{tan}⁡({180}^{∘})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The angle ${180}^{∘}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/ed/edad8c833f65c54453fe40a326cbf9394c263213934447872b530a8e4fb86de1.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${180}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(-1,0).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡({180}^{∘})=-1.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({180}^{∘})=0.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{180}^{∘}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({180}^{∘})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡({180}^{∘})}=\\frac{0}{-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{tan}⁡({180}^{∘})=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186058168 :answer-field/correct "answer-533414d9-c68a-5542-8173-5a56bddfe3ce" :answer-field/choices ["answer-533414d9-c68a-5542-8173-5a56bddfe3ce"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-533414d9-c68a-5542-8173-5a56bddfe3ce" :answer/id #uuid "533414d9-c68a-5542-8173-5a56bddfe3ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186060338 :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "The angle $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/ed/edad8c833f65c54453fe40a326cbf9394c263213934447872b530a8e4fb86de1.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,-1).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{-1}{0}.\n$$\n\nHowever, division by zero is undefined. Therefore, $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})$ is undefined." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186058650 :answer-field/correct 17592186058654 :answer-field/choices ["answer-00ffd31e-38fd-58cd-96f0-3396e6596239"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00ffd31e-38fd-58cd-96f0-3396e6596239" :answer/id #uuid "00ffd31e-38fd-58cd-96f0-3396e6596239" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-621a7d94-aa15-5da2-a86e-6e37bead83f1" :question/id #uuid "621a7d94-aa15-5da2-a86e-6e37bead83f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-123585" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡({180}^{∘})+\\operatorname{csc}⁡({90}^{∘}).$" :question/worked-solution "The angles ${180}^{∘}$ and ${90}^{∘}$ are quadrantal angles. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\n- Let's first consider the angle ${180}^{∘}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle ${180}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(-1,0).$  Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡({180}^{∘})=-1.$ Therefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡({180}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡({180}^{∘})}=\\frac{1}{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\n- Now, let's consider the angle ${90}^{∘}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle ${90}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$  Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({90}^{∘})=1.$ Therefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡({90}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({90}^{∘})}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡({180}^{∘})+\\operatorname{csc}⁡({90}^{∘})=-1+1=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-99de1166-6614-59dc-ba88-9b3adbcbf5e8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-99de1166-6614-59dc-ba88-9b3adbcbf5e8" :answer-field/id #uuid "99de1166-6614-59dc-ba88-9b3adbcbf5e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-16e33752-dbec-5be0-abda-0b23cc352083" :answer-field/choices ["answer-65a2005c-6f9c-5b3b-92b5-b91a280aa805" "answer-7ac225ab-a108-5cf1-b663-e25ab4138d7b" "answer-16e33752-dbec-5be0-abda-0b23cc352083" "answer-fc8fae09-f797-5025-b37c-64365f7ed7cd" "answer-80712ae4-c0c2-56af-a31a-939b00f59f46"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65a2005c-6f9c-5b3b-92b5-b91a280aa805" :answer/id #uuid "65a2005c-6f9c-5b3b-92b5-b91a280aa805" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ac225ab-a108-5cf1-b663-e25ab4138d7b" :answer/id #uuid "7ac225ab-a108-5cf1-b663-e25ab4138d7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16e33752-dbec-5be0-abda-0b23cc352083" :answer/id #uuid "16e33752-dbec-5be0-abda-0b23cc352083" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc8fae09-f797-5025-b37c-64365f7ed7cd" :answer/id #uuid "fc8fae09-f797-5025-b37c-64365f7ed7cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80712ae4-c0c2-56af-a31a-939b00f59f46" :answer/id #uuid "80712ae4-c0c2-56af-a31a-939b00f59f46" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Undefined" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5dc0c374-75b0-5038-a236-6349d3712634" :question/id #uuid "5dc0c374-75b0-5038-a236-6349d3712634" :question/math-academy-id "q-23418" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "The angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{1}{0}.\n$$\n\nHowever, division by zero is undefined. Therefore, $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is undefined." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2ba2b05c-984d-5188-ba82-e7eff754bb83"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2ba2b05c-984d-5188-ba82-e7eff754bb83" :answer-field/id #uuid "2ba2b05c-984d-5188-ba82-e7eff754bb83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-08429320-9580-541b-a6d9-31a45aa346b8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b00a2584-97c8-50c5-81c4-5f4529530f60" "answer-42d752c4-1ced-5442-9d14-2e028d4482e2" "answer-a04922be-e134-5966-b10b-e756217fb61e" "answer-5b0f6439-02a9-5ffc-b967-796d14fd99c9" "answer-08429320-9580-541b-a6d9-31a45aa346b8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b00a2584-97c8-50c5-81c4-5f4529530f60" :answer/id #uuid "b00a2584-97c8-50c5-81c4-5f4529530f60" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-42d752c4-1ced-5442-9d14-2e028d4482e2" :answer/id #uuid "42d752c4-1ced-5442-9d14-2e028d4482e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a04922be-e134-5966-b10b-e756217fb61e" :answer/id #uuid "a04922be-e134-5966-b10b-e756217fb61e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b0f6439-02a9-5ffc-b967-796d14fd99c9" :answer/id #uuid "5b0f6439-02a9-5ffc-b967-796d14fd99c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-08429320-9580-541b-a6d9-31a45aa346b8" :answer/id #uuid "08429320-9580-541b-a6d9-31a45aa346b8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Undefined" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186060905 :tutorial/content "A **quadrantal angle** is an angle whose terminal side lies on the $x$ or $y$-axis. \nThe unit circle below illustrates the quadrantal angles that lie on the range $[0^\\circ, 360^\\circ).$\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nWe can identify four points on the unit circle above,\n\n$$\n(1,0), \\quad (0,1), \\quad (-1,0), \\quad (0,-1),\n$$\n\nthat correspond to the angles\n\n$$\n0^\\circ, \\quad 90^\\circ,\\quad 180^\\circ, \\quad 270^\\circ,\n$$\n\nin degrees, or\n\n$$\n0, \\quad \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\quad \\pi, \\quad \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},\n$$\n\nin radians.\n\nWe can use these points to find cosine and sine of quadrantal angles. First, recall that a point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nFor example, if we are asked to find the value of $\\cos(\\pi),$ then we just look at the $\\color{blue}x$-coordinate of the corresponding point. The point is $({\\color{blue}{-1}},0),$ and therefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\cos(\\pi)={\\color{blue}{-1}}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059944 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}] "assertions" [[17592186059515 :question/problem "Find the value of $\\sin{270^\\circ}.$"] [17592186059515 :question/worked-solution "The angle $270^\\circ$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $270^\\circ$ corresponds to the point $(0,-1).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\sin{270^\\circ} = -1.$"] [17592186059516 :question/problem "Find the value of $\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right).$"] [17592186059516 :question/worked-solution "The angle $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,-1).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) = 0.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) = -1.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\tan{\\theta} = \\dfrac{\\sin \\theta}{\\cos\\theta},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right)}{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right)} = \\dfrac{-1}{0}.\n$$\n\nHowever, division by zero is undefined. Therefore, $\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right)$ is undefined."] [17592186059517 :question/problem "Find the value of $\\csc \\left(0\\right).$"] [17592186059517 :question/worked-solution "The angle $0$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/ed/edad8c833f65c54453fe40a326cbf9394c263213934447872b530a8e4fb86de1.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $0$ corresponds to the point $(1,0).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\sin{\\left(0\\right)} = 0.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\csc{\\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\csc{\\left(0\\right)} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{\\left(0\\right)}} = \\dfrac{1}{0}.\n$$\n\nHowever, division by zero is undefined. Therefore, $\\csc\\left(0\\right)$ is undefined."] [17592186060255 :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡({90}^{∘})=${{field-1}}"] [17592186060255 :question/worked-solution "The angle ${90}^{∘}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/ed/edad8c833f65c54453fe40a326cbf9394c263213934447872b530a8e4fb86de1.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${90}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({90}^{∘})=1.$"] [17592186060255 :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-8f074172-16a6-5de2-9d7b-00f0f7fba825" :answer/id #uuid "8f074172-16a6-5de2-9d7b-00f0f7fba825"] ["answer-8f074172-16a6-5de2-9d7b-00f0f7fba825" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-8f074172-16a6-5de2-9d7b-00f0f7fba825" :answer/value "1"] [17592186058167 :answer-field/correct "answer-8f074172-16a6-5de2-9d7b-00f0f7fba825"] [17592186058167 :answer-field/choices "answer-8f074172-16a6-5de2-9d7b-00f0f7fba825"] ["question-29efc143-8ef6-514b-b08b-b09cf3c6dccf" :question/id #uuid "29efc143-8ef6-514b-b08b-b09cf3c6dccf"] ["question-29efc143-8ef6-514b-b08b-b09cf3c6dccf" :question/math-academy-id "q-64861"] ["question-29efc143-8ef6-514b-b08b-b09cf3c6dccf" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$"] ["question-29efc143-8ef6-514b-b08b-b09cf3c6dccf" :question/worked-solution "The angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.$"] ["question-29efc143-8ef6-514b-b08b-b09cf3c6dccf" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-d78da839-c447-5f6a-b9f4-52fee3e7c783" :answer-field/id #uuid "d78da839-c447-5f6a-b9f4-52fee3e7c783"] ["answer-bc748a1d-aa55-53b1-8727-38e8aeaf186e" :answer/id #uuid "bc748a1d-aa55-53b1-8727-38e8aeaf186e"] ["answer-bc748a1d-aa55-53b1-8727-38e8aeaf186e" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-bc748a1d-aa55-53b1-8727-38e8aeaf186e" :answer/value "-1"] ["answer-5e88955e-5bc1-5000-8512-3eeafc61d0f6" :answer/id #uuid "5e88955e-5bc1-5000-8512-3eeafc61d0f6"] ["answer-5e88955e-5bc1-5000-8512-3eeafc61d0f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-5e88955e-5bc1-5000-8512-3eeafc61d0f6" :answer/value "0"] ["answer-6238dcb6-e730-5761-a8fc-314e6a8b5732" :answer/id #uuid "6238dcb6-e730-5761-a8fc-314e6a8b5732"] ["answer-6238dcb6-e730-5761-a8fc-314e6a8b5732" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-6238dcb6-e730-5761-a8fc-314e6a8b5732" :answer/value "1"] ["answer-751d8865-5818-58ec-a5f2-b7595be506a7" :answer/id #uuid "751d8865-5818-58ec-a5f2-b7595be506a7"] ["answer-751d8865-5818-58ec-a5f2-b7595be506a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-751d8865-5818-58ec-a5f2-b7595be506a7" :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] ["answer-a54b68c4-6f83-5b6d-a8d5-c61dfd48387e" :answer/id #uuid "a54b68c4-6f83-5b6d-a8d5-c61dfd48387e"] ["answer-a54b68c4-6f83-5b6d-a8d5-c61dfd48387e" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-a54b68c4-6f83-5b6d-a8d5-c61dfd48387e" :answer/value "Undefined"] ["answer-field-d78da839-c447-5f6a-b9f4-52fee3e7c783" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-d78da839-c447-5f6a-b9f4-52fee3e7c783" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-d78da839-c447-5f6a-b9f4-52fee3e7c783" :answer-field/correct "answer-6238dcb6-e730-5761-a8fc-314e6a8b5732"] ["answer-field-d78da839-c447-5f6a-b9f4-52fee3e7c783" :answer-field/choices "answer-bc748a1d-aa55-53b1-8727-38e8aeaf186e"] ["answer-field-d78da839-c447-5f6a-b9f4-52fee3e7c783" :answer-field/choices "answer-5e88955e-5bc1-5000-8512-3eeafc61d0f6"] ["answer-field-d78da839-c447-5f6a-b9f4-52fee3e7c783" :answer-field/choices "answer-6238dcb6-e730-5761-a8fc-314e6a8b5732"] ["answer-field-d78da839-c447-5f6a-b9f4-52fee3e7c783" :answer-field/choices "answer-751d8865-5818-58ec-a5f2-b7595be506a7"] ["answer-field-d78da839-c447-5f6a-b9f4-52fee3e7c783" :answer-field/choices "answer-a54b68c4-6f83-5b6d-a8d5-c61dfd48387e"] ["question-29efc143-8ef6-514b-b08b-b09cf3c6dccf" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-d78da839-c447-5f6a-b9f4-52fee3e7c783"] [17592186059840 :knowledge-point/questions "question-29efc143-8ef6-514b-b08b-b09cf3c6dccf"] [17592186060256 :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡({180}^{∘}).$\n\n$\\operatorname{tan}⁡({180}^{∘})=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186060256 :question/worked-solution "The angle ${180}^{∘}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/ed/edad8c833f65c54453fe40a326cbf9394c263213934447872b530a8e4fb86de1.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${180}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(-1,0).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡({180}^{∘})=-1.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({180}^{∘})=0.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{180}^{∘}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({180}^{∘})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡({180}^{∘})}=\\frac{0}{-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{tan}⁡({180}^{∘})=0.$"] [17592186060256 :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-533414d9-c68a-5542-8173-5a56bddfe3ce" :answer/id #uuid "533414d9-c68a-5542-8173-5a56bddfe3ce"] ["answer-533414d9-c68a-5542-8173-5a56bddfe3ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-533414d9-c68a-5542-8173-5a56bddfe3ce" :answer/value "0"] [17592186058168 :answer-field/correct "answer-533414d9-c68a-5542-8173-5a56bddfe3ce"] [17592186058168 :answer-field/choices "answer-533414d9-c68a-5542-8173-5a56bddfe3ce"] [17592186060338 :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).$"] [17592186060338 :question/worked-solution "The angle $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/ed/edad8c833f65c54453fe40a326cbf9394c263213934447872b530a8e4fb86de1.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,-1).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{-1}{0}.\n$$\n\nHowever, division by zero is undefined. Therefore, $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})$ is undefined."] [17592186060338 :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-00ffd31e-38fd-58cd-96f0-3396e6596239" :answer/id #uuid "00ffd31e-38fd-58cd-96f0-3396e6596239"] ["answer-00ffd31e-38fd-58cd-96f0-3396e6596239" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-00ffd31e-38fd-58cd-96f0-3396e6596239" :answer/value "\\pi "] [17592186058650 :answer-field/correct 17592186058654] [17592186058650 :answer-field/choices "answer-00ffd31e-38fd-58cd-96f0-3396e6596239"] ["question-621a7d94-aa15-5da2-a86e-6e37bead83f1" :question/id #uuid "621a7d94-aa15-5da2-a86e-6e37bead83f1"] ["question-621a7d94-aa15-5da2-a86e-6e37bead83f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-123585"] ["question-621a7d94-aa15-5da2-a86e-6e37bead83f1" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡({180}^{∘})+\\operatorname{csc}⁡({90}^{∘}).$"] ["question-621a7d94-aa15-5da2-a86e-6e37bead83f1" :question/worked-solution "The angles ${180}^{∘}$ and ${90}^{∘}$ are quadrantal angles. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\n- Let's first consider the angle ${180}^{∘}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle ${180}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(-1,0).$  Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡({180}^{∘})=-1.$ Therefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡({180}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡({180}^{∘})}=\\frac{1}{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\n- Now, let's consider the angle ${90}^{∘}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle ${90}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$  Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({90}^{∘})=1.$ Therefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡({90}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({90}^{∘})}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡({180}^{∘})+\\operatorname{csc}⁡({90}^{∘})=-1+1=0.\n$$"] ["question-621a7d94-aa15-5da2-a86e-6e37bead83f1" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-99de1166-6614-59dc-ba88-9b3adbcbf5e8" :answer-field/id #uuid "99de1166-6614-59dc-ba88-9b3adbcbf5e8"] ["answer-65a2005c-6f9c-5b3b-92b5-b91a280aa805" :answer/id #uuid "65a2005c-6f9c-5b3b-92b5-b91a280aa805"] ["answer-65a2005c-6f9c-5b3b-92b5-b91a280aa805" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-65a2005c-6f9c-5b3b-92b5-b91a280aa805" :answer/value "-1"] ["answer-7ac225ab-a108-5cf1-b663-e25ab4138d7b" :answer/id #uuid "7ac225ab-a108-5cf1-b663-e25ab4138d7b"] ["answer-7ac225ab-a108-5cf1-b663-e25ab4138d7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-7ac225ab-a108-5cf1-b663-e25ab4138d7b" :answer/value "-2"] ["answer-16e33752-dbec-5be0-abda-0b23cc352083" :answer/id #uuid "16e33752-dbec-5be0-abda-0b23cc352083"] ["answer-16e33752-dbec-5be0-abda-0b23cc352083" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-16e33752-dbec-5be0-abda-0b23cc352083" :answer/value "0"] ["answer-fc8fae09-f797-5025-b37c-64365f7ed7cd" :answer/id #uuid "fc8fae09-f797-5025-b37c-64365f7ed7cd"] ["answer-fc8fae09-f797-5025-b37c-64365f7ed7cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-fc8fae09-f797-5025-b37c-64365f7ed7cd" :answer/value "1"] ["answer-80712ae4-c0c2-56af-a31a-939b00f59f46" :answer/id #uuid "80712ae4-c0c2-56af-a31a-939b00f59f46"] ["answer-80712ae4-c0c2-56af-a31a-939b00f59f46" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-80712ae4-c0c2-56af-a31a-939b00f59f46" :answer/value "Undefined"] ["answer-field-99de1166-6614-59dc-ba88-9b3adbcbf5e8" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-99de1166-6614-59dc-ba88-9b3adbcbf5e8" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-99de1166-6614-59dc-ba88-9b3adbcbf5e8" :answer-field/correct "answer-16e33752-dbec-5be0-abda-0b23cc352083"] ["answer-field-99de1166-6614-59dc-ba88-9b3adbcbf5e8" :answer-field/choices "answer-65a2005c-6f9c-5b3b-92b5-b91a280aa805"] ["answer-field-99de1166-6614-59dc-ba88-9b3adbcbf5e8" :answer-field/choices "answer-7ac225ab-a108-5cf1-b663-e25ab4138d7b"] ["answer-field-99de1166-6614-59dc-ba88-9b3adbcbf5e8" :answer-field/choices "answer-16e33752-dbec-5be0-abda-0b23cc352083"] ["answer-field-99de1166-6614-59dc-ba88-9b3adbcbf5e8" :answer-field/choices "answer-fc8fae09-f797-5025-b37c-64365f7ed7cd"] ["answer-field-99de1166-6614-59dc-ba88-9b3adbcbf5e8" :answer-field/choices "answer-80712ae4-c0c2-56af-a31a-939b00f59f46"] ["question-621a7d94-aa15-5da2-a86e-6e37bead83f1" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-99de1166-6614-59dc-ba88-9b3adbcbf5e8"] [17592186059842 :knowledge-point/questions "question-621a7d94-aa15-5da2-a86e-6e37bead83f1"] ["question-5dc0c374-75b0-5038-a236-6349d3712634" :question/id #uuid "5dc0c374-75b0-5038-a236-6349d3712634"] ["question-5dc0c374-75b0-5038-a236-6349d3712634" :question/math-academy-id "q-23418"] ["question-5dc0c374-75b0-5038-a236-6349d3712634" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$"] ["question-5dc0c374-75b0-5038-a236-6349d3712634" :question/worked-solution "The angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{1}{0}.\n$$\n\nHowever, division by zero is undefined. Therefore, $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is undefined."] ["question-5dc0c374-75b0-5038-a236-6349d3712634" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-2ba2b05c-984d-5188-ba82-e7eff754bb83" :answer-field/id #uuid "2ba2b05c-984d-5188-ba82-e7eff754bb83"] ["answer-b00a2584-97c8-50c5-81c4-5f4529530f60" :answer/id #uuid "b00a2584-97c8-50c5-81c4-5f4529530f60"] ["answer-b00a2584-97c8-50c5-81c4-5f4529530f60" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-b00a2584-97c8-50c5-81c4-5f4529530f60" :answer/value "-1"] ["answer-42d752c4-1ced-5442-9d14-2e028d4482e2" :answer/id #uuid "42d752c4-1ced-5442-9d14-2e028d4482e2"] ["answer-42d752c4-1ced-5442-9d14-2e028d4482e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-42d752c4-1ced-5442-9d14-2e028d4482e2" :answer/value "0"] ["answer-a04922be-e134-5966-b10b-e756217fb61e" :answer/id #uuid "a04922be-e134-5966-b10b-e756217fb61e"] ["answer-a04922be-e134-5966-b10b-e756217fb61e" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-a04922be-e134-5966-b10b-e756217fb61e" :answer/value "1"] ["answer-5b0f6439-02a9-5ffc-b967-796d14fd99c9" :answer/id #uuid "5b0f6439-02a9-5ffc-b967-796d14fd99c9"] ["answer-5b0f6439-02a9-5ffc-b967-796d14fd99c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-5b0f6439-02a9-5ffc-b967-796d14fd99c9" :answer/value "2"] ["answer-08429320-9580-541b-a6d9-31a45aa346b8" :answer/id #uuid "08429320-9580-541b-a6d9-31a45aa346b8"] ["answer-08429320-9580-541b-a6d9-31a45aa346b8" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-08429320-9580-541b-a6d9-31a45aa346b8" :answer/value "Undefined"] ["answer-field-2ba2b05c-984d-5188-ba82-e7eff754bb83" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-2ba2b05c-984d-5188-ba82-e7eff754bb83" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-2ba2b05c-984d-5188-ba82-e7eff754bb83" :answer-field/correct "answer-08429320-9580-541b-a6d9-31a45aa346b8"] ["answer-field-2ba2b05c-984d-5188-ba82-e7eff754bb83" :answer-field/choices "answer-b00a2584-97c8-50c5-81c4-5f4529530f60"] ["answer-field-2ba2b05c-984d-5188-ba82-e7eff754bb83" :answer-field/choices "answer-42d752c4-1ced-5442-9d14-2e028d4482e2"] ["answer-field-2ba2b05c-984d-5188-ba82-e7eff754bb83" :answer-field/choices "answer-a04922be-e134-5966-b10b-e756217fb61e"] ["answer-field-2ba2b05c-984d-5188-ba82-e7eff754bb83" :answer-field/choices "answer-5b0f6439-02a9-5ffc-b967-796d14fd99c9"] ["answer-field-2ba2b05c-984d-5188-ba82-e7eff754bb83" :answer-field/choices "answer-08429320-9580-541b-a6d9-31a45aa346b8"] ["question-5dc0c374-75b0-5038-a236-6349d3712634" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-2ba2b05c-984d-5188-ba82-e7eff754bb83"] [17592186059842 :knowledge-point/questions "question-5dc0c374-75b0-5038-a236-6349d3712634"] [17592186060905 :tutorial/content "A **quadrantal angle** is an angle whose terminal side lies on the $x$ or $y$-axis. \nThe unit circle below illustrates the quadrantal angles that lie on the range $[0^\\circ, 360^\\circ).$\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nWe can identify four points on the unit circle above,\n\n$$\n(1,0), \\quad (0,1), \\quad (-1,0), \\quad (0,-1),\n$$\n\nthat correspond to the angles\n\n$$\n0^\\circ, \\quad 90^\\circ,\\quad 180^\\circ, \\quad 270^\\circ,\n$$\n\nin degrees, or\n\n$$\n0, \\quad \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\quad \\pi, \\quad \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},\n$$\n\nin radians.\n\nWe can use these points to find cosine and sine of quadrantal angles. First, recall that a point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nFor example, if we are asked to find the value of $\\cos(\\pi),$ then we just look at the $\\color{blue}x$-coordinate of the corresponding point. The point is $({\\color{blue}{-1}},0),$ and therefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\cos(\\pi)={\\color{blue}{-1}}.\n$$"] [17592186059944 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0]] "retractions" [[17592186059515 :question/problem "Find the value of $\\sin{270^\\circ}$."] [17592186059515 :question/worked-solution "The angle $270^\\circ$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $270^\\circ$ corresponds to the point $(0,-1)$.\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin{270^\\circ} = -1\n$$"] [17592186059516 :question/problem "Find the value of $\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right)$."] [17592186059516 :question/worked-solution "The angle\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nis a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\ncorresponds to the point $(0,-1)$.\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) = 0$.\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) = -1$.\n\nTherefore, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\tan{\\theta} = \\dfrac{\\sin \\theta}{\\cos\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right)}{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right)} = \\dfrac{-1}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, division by zero is undefined. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nis undefined."] [17592186059517 :question/problem "Find the value of $\\sec \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$."] [17592186059517 :question/worked-solution "The angle\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\pi} 2\n$$\n\nis a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\ncorresponds to the point $(0,1)$.\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac {\\pi} 2\\right)} = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\sec{\\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\sec{\\left(\\dfrac {\\pi} 2\\right)} = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac {\\pi} 2\\right)}} = \\dfrac{1}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, division by zero is undefined. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\sec\\left(\\dfrac {\\pi} 2\\right)\n$$\n\nis undefined."] [17592186060255 :question/problem "$\\sin (90^{∘}) =$ {{field-1}}"] [17592186060256 :question/problem "Find the value of $\\tan (180^{∘})$.$\\tan (180^{∘}) =$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186060338 :question/problem "Find the value of $\\tan (\\frac{3π}{2})$."] [17592186058650 :answer-field/choices 17592186058653] [17592186060905 :tutorial/content "A **quadrantal angle** is an angle whose terminal side lies on the $x$ or $y$-axis.\nThe unit circle below illustrates the quadrantal angles that lie on the range $[0^\\circ, 360^\\circ)$.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nWe can identify four points on the unit circle above,\n\n$$\n(1,0), \\quad (0,1), \\quad (-1,0), \\quad (0,-1)\n$$\n\nthat correspond to the angles\n\n$$\n0^\\circ, \\quad 90^\\circ,\\quad 180^\\circ, \\quad 270^\\circ\n$$\n\nin degrees, or\n\n$$\n0, \\quad \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\quad \\pi, \\quad \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nin radians.\n\nWe can use these points to find cosine and sine of quadrantal angles. First, recall that a point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nFor example, if we are asked to find the value of $\\cos(\\pi)$, then we just look at the $\\color{blue}x$-coordinate of the corresponding point. The point is $({\\color{blue}{-1}},0)$, and therefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\cos(\\pi)={\\color{blue}{-1}}\n$$"]]}]